But perhaps the key is to consider that stellar activity can mimic planet signals. The Ha and CaII lines (which are indicators of chromospheric activity) show a peak at 28.1 days. That might be due to stellar rotation or activity cycles. Since the star's rotational velocity is 0.9 km/s, the rotational period of the star would be the circumference divided by the rotational velocity. The circumference is 2πR. The star has the same radius as the Sun, which is about 700,000 km. So circumference is about 2 * π * 7e5 ≈ 4.4e6 km. The rotational velocity is 0.9 km/s. So the rotational period would be 4.4e6 km / 0.9 km/s ≈ 4.9e6 seconds. Converting seconds to days: 4.9e6 / (60*60*24) ≈ 4.9e6 / 86400 ≈ 56.7 days. So the star's rotational period is about 56.7 days. That's very close to the 56.2 days peak in the RV data. That suggests that the 56.2 days peak in the RV data might be due to stellar rotation, which would be an alias or a contamination from stellar activity. However, the peak at 56.2 days is in the RV data, while the stellar rotation period is about 56.7 days. That's very close, so it's possible that the 56.2 day peak is due to the star's rotation, which would be a false positive. However, the question says that the star's rotational axis is inclined by 90 degrees. Wait, but if the rotational axis is inclined by 90 degrees, that would mean that the star is rotating edge-on, so the projected rotational velocity vsin(i) is equal to the actual rotational velocity. Wait, the inclination of the rotational axis being 90 degrees would mean that the star's rotation is perpendicular to our line of sight, so the vsin(i) is equal to the actual rotational velocity. Wait, but the vsin(i) is 0.9 km/s. So if the inclination is 90 degrees, then sin(i) is 1, so the actual rotational velocity is 0.9 km/s. Then, the rotational period would be as I calculated before, around 56.7 days. Therefore, the 56.2 day peak in the RV data is very close to the star's rotational period. This would