But then the other substituents: ortho to the carboxylic acid (position 1) are hydroxyl and dimethylamino. Ortho to position 1 would be positions 2 and 6. So those two groups are at 2 and 6. Also, para to the carboxylic acid (position 1) is position 4, which has a methoxy group. So position 4 is methoxy. Additionally, methoxy (position 4) and hydroxyl (say position 2 or 6) are both ortho to the nitrile. The nitrile is at position 3 or 5. Let me check.

Let me assume that the carboxylic acid is at position 1. Then, the other two groups (carbaldehyde and cyano) are at positions 3 and 5. Let's say the cyano is at position 3, and the carbaldehyde is at position 5. Wait, but the question says the methoxy and hydroxyl are both ortho to the nitrile. The nitrile is at position 3. Ortho to position 3 is positions 2 and 4. The methoxy is at position 4. So if the nitrile is at position 3, then methoxy (position 4) is ortho to it. Also, the hydroxyl group must be ortho to the nitrile. So hydroxyl would be at position 2. Then, the hydroxyl is at position 2 (ortho to nitrile at 3). The dimethylamino is at position 6 (since ortho to carboxylic acid at 1 are positions 2 and 6; hydroxyl is at 2, so dimethylamino is at 6). 

