Well, 2 * 8.7 = 17.4, 0.7 * 8.7 = 6.09, 0.003 * 8.7 = 0.0261. So total is 17.4 + 6.09 = 23.49 + 0.0261 ≈ 23.5161. So τ ≈ 23.5161 × 10⁻⁵ s = 2.35161 × 10⁻⁴ s. Let me note that as τ ≈ 2.35 × 10⁻⁴ seconds.

Now, the number of particles remaining after traveling a distance d is given by N = N₀ exp(-d / (v τ)), where v is the velocity of the particles. Wait, but actually, the decay is exponential with time, so the number of particles remaining is N = N₀ exp(-t / τ), where t is the time elapsed in the lab frame. But since the particles are moving at velocity v, the time t is related to the distance d by t = d / v. Therefore, substituting, N = N₀ exp(-d / (v τ)). But since τ is the dilated lifetime, which is γ τ₀, and v is the velocity of the particle.

