Wait, C6 is part of the three-membered ring (C1-C2-C6). So C6 is connected to C1 and C2. If there's no substituent on C6, it would be a CH2 group? No, because it's connected to two other carbons. So it would be CH2. But if it has a substituent, like the ketone, it would be different. 

In our case, the ketone is at position 6. So, C6 is part of the three-membered ring and has a ketone group. So, the structure at C6 would be a carbonyl (C=O). Therefore, C6 is a CH group (since it's connected to C1, C2, and the carbonyl). 

So, in the five-membered ring (C1, C3, C4, C5, C2), each of these carbons (except the bridgeheads) would be CH2 groups. Wait, C1 is a bridgehead, connected to C3 and C6. If there's no substituent on C1, it would be CH. Similarly for C2. 

