Starting with reaction A: dimethyl malonate + methyl (E)-3-(p-tolyl)acrylate with NaOEt and EtOH. Michael reaction involves a nucleophile attacking the β-carbon of an α,β-unsaturated carbonyl. The nucleophile here is dimethyl malonate, which is a common Michael acceptor? Wait, no. Wait, the Michael reaction is when a nucleophile adds to the β-carbon of an α,β-unsaturated carbonyl. Wait, the nucleophile would be the malonate, and the electrophile is the acrylate. Wait, but the acrylate has the double bond adjacent to the carbonyl, making it the Michael acceptor. So the malonate (which is a nucleophile in this case) would attack the β-carbon of the acrylate. 

