• Protein MAB13 expressed in HEK293 cells at 37°C: Hydrodynamic radius – 7.1 nm (95% intensity distribution), 30 nm (5% intensity distribution).

• Protein MAB13 expressed in Escherichia coli at 37°C as an N-terminal fusion with GFP (green fluorescent protein): Hydrodynamic radius – 30 nm (70% intensity distribution), 55 nm (30% intensity distribution).

• Protein MAB13 expressed in Escherichia coli at 18°C as an N-terminal fusion with MBP (maltose binding protein): Hydrodynamic radius – 7.1 nm (60% intensity distribution), 30 nm (30% intensity distribution), 55 nm (10% intensity distribution).

Based on the presented data, choose the correct answer:","[""Fusion of another protein to the N-terminal part of MAB13 does not help in the folding process of MAB13."", ""Both lower expression temperature and fusion to GFP improve the quality of MAB13."", ""Fusion to MBP improves the folding process of MAB13; MAB13 folds properly in Escherichia coli at 37\u00b0C."", ""Adding a fusion of a protein to the N-terminal end of MAB13 improves the folding process of MAB13; MAB13 can fold properly at 37\u00b0C."", ""Both GFP and HP70 do not facilitate the folding of MAB13."", ""HP70 facilitates the folding process of MAB13 at 18\u00b0C and 37\u00b0C, MBP and lower temperature improve the folding process of MAB13.""]",D,"Okay, let's try to figure this out. The question is about protein aggregation and how different conditions affect the folding of MAB13. The options are A to F, and I need to pick the correct one based on the DLS data provided.

First, I need to recall what dynamic light scattering (DLS) measures. DLS determines the hydrodynamic radius of particles in solution, which can indicate the size of protein aggregates. A smaller hydrodynamic radius suggests the protein is properly folded and not aggregated, while larger sizes indicate aggregation.

