Then there's the co-expression with HP70. At 18°C, when co-expressed with HP70, the hydrodynamic radius is 7.1 nm (70%) and 30 nm (30%). Then another entry for co-expression with HP70 at 18°C shows 7.1 nm (85%) and 30 nm (15%). Wait, maybe that's a typo? Or maybe different conditions? Wait, the user lists two entries for co-expression with HP70 at 18°C. The first one says 7.1 nm (70%) and 30 nm (30%), the second one says 7.1 nm (85%) and 30 nm (15%). Maybe that's a mistake in the data, or perhaps different batches? But assuming that the second one is more accurate, with higher percentage of 7.1 nm, which is better. So co-expressing with HP70 at 18°C improves the folding, reducing the amount of aggregated protein (30 nm) to 15% in the second entry.

Now, looking at the fusion proteins. When MAB13 is expressed as an N-terminal fusion with GFP at 37°C, the hydrodynamic radius is the same as the regular 37°C expression: 30 nm (70%) and 55 nm (30%). So the GFP fusion didn't help here. But when expressed with MBP at 18°C, the hydrodynamic radii are 7.1 nm (60%), 30 nm (30%), and 55 nm (10%). So even with MBP fusion, there's still some aggregation (30 nm and 10% at 55 nm), but more of the protein is in the 7.1 nm size. So MBP fusion at 18°C seems to help, but not as much as HP70 co-expression or HEK293 cells at 37°C.

The HEK293 cells at 37°C give 95% at 7.1 nm, which is excellent. So that's a good result. So lower temperature (18°C) and co-expression with HP70 seem to help, and HEK293 cells at 37°C also help. Also, MBP fusion at 18°C helps, but not as much as HP70.

