Now, let me consider the mapping from the unit sphere in the span of y_i's to ℝ^n. Let me think of the coordinates (⟨y₁, s⟩, ..., ⟨y_n, s⟩). This is a linear map from the n-dimensional space to ℝ^n. The squared norms of these would be quadratic. However, the set of all possible (⟨y₁, s⟩, ..., ⟨y_n, s⟩) for ||s||=1 is the image of the unit sphere under a linear map, which is an ellipsoid. Wait, because if you have a linear map L: ℝ^n → ℝ^n, then the image of the unit sphere under L is an ellipsoid if and only if L is invertible. Since the y_i's are linearly independent, the Gram matrix G is invertible, so the linear map from s to the vector ⟨y₁,s⟩, ..., ⟨y_n,s⟩ is invertible? Wait, not exactly. Wait, the linear map here is s ↦ (⟨y₁, s⟩, ..., ⟨y_n, s⟩). Let me denote this as a matrix multiplication. If I have s expressed as a vector in the basis {y₁, ..., y_n}, then the inner products ⟨y_i, s⟩ would be the coordinates of s in the dual basis? Not sure. Alternatively, if I think of the vectors y_i as columns of a matrix Y, then the inner product ⟨y_i, s⟩ is the i-th entry of Y^T s. Wait, if Y is a matrix whose columns are y₁, ..., y_n, then Y^T s is a vector where each entry is ⟨y_i, s⟩. Therefore, the mapping from s to Y^T s is a linear transformation. The image of the unit sphere under this transformation is an ellipsoid if Y^T is full rank, which it is since the y_i are linearly independent. Therefore, the set {Y^T s | ||s|| = 1} is an ellipsoid in ℝ^n. But our set S is not exactly that. Our set S is {(|⟨y₁, s⟩|², ..., |⟨y_n, s⟩|²) | ||s|| = 1}. So it's the image under the map that takes each coordinate to its square, and also takes absolute value. 

