Option C is Old English > Modern English > German > Russian. If that's the case, then Old English is the most transparent, then Modern English, then German, then Russian. But I'm not sure if that's right. Another option is B: German > Russian > Modern English > Old English. That seems counterintuitive because Old English might be more transparent than Modern English.

Alternatively, if Russian has more transparency than German, then maybe D: Russian > German > Old English > Modern English. But I'm not sure. Wait, another angle: semantic transparency could also relate to the predictability of word meanings. For example, in agglutinative languages like Turkish, words are formed by adding affixes, making the meaning more transparent. But English is analytic, relying more on word order and function words. However, Old English was more synthetic, so maybe more transparent. Russian is synthetic too, with cases. German as well.

But if we consider the degree of syntheticity, Old English might be more synthetic than Modern English. So, perhaps Old English has higher transparency than Modern English. Then, German and Russian might be in between. But how do German and Russian compare? Russian has more cases (six or seven?) compared to German's four. So maybe Russian is more synthetic and thus more transparent. Then, the order might be Old English > German > Russian > Modern English. But that's not one of the options. Let me check the options again.

