So if Old English is more transparent than Modern English, then the correct sequence should have Old English before Modern English. Let's check the options. C, D, and E have Old English after Modern English. Wait, no. Option C is Old English > Modern English > German > Russian. That's Old English first, then Modern English, then German, then Russian. If that's the case, then that would be the order of most to least transparent. But why would German be more transparent than Russian? If Russian has more cases, maybe it's more transparent. But I'm not sure. Alternatively, maybe Müller-Gotama's theory considers something else.

Wait, maybe I should think about the theory. Since I don't know Müller-Gotama's work, perhaps this is a reference to a known theory. Wait, Müller-Gotama is actually a name that's sometimes used in linguistics, but I might be confusing with someone else. Alternatively, maybe it's a trick question where the answer is based on the fact that Old English is more transparent than Modern English, German is more than Modern English, and Russian more than German. But I'm not sure.

Alternatively, considering that Modern English has more loanwords and irregularities, so less transparent. Old English is more regular. German and Russian are more synthetic. Between German and Russian, which is more transparent? If Russian is more synthetic (more cases), then maybe higher transparency. So the order would be Old English > German > Russian > Modern English. But none of the options have that. Option C is Old English > Modern English > German > Russian. That would put Modern English higher than German and Russian, which conflicts with that.

Alternatively, maybe the order is German > Russian > Old English > Modern English. But that's not an option. Wait, the options given are:

A) Modern English>Old English>Russian>German

