But I'm not confident. Alternatively, maybe Müller-Gotama's theory is about the degree of morphological transparency, and in that case, the order would be from more to less transparent: Old English (high), German (medium), Russian (medium), Modern English (low). But that's not matching any options. Alternatively, maybe German is higher than Russian. 

Alternatively, considering that German is a Germanic language with more cases than Modern English, but fewer than Russian. So maybe German is more transparent than Modern English, but less than Old English and Russian. If Russian is more transparent than German, then the order would be Old English > Russian > German > Modern English. But that's not an option either. The closest is C: Old English > Modern English > German > Russian. Which is not matching.

Alternatively, if the order is Old English > German > Russian > Modern English, but none of the options have that. So perhaps the correct answer is C, assuming that Modern English is more transparent than German and Russian. But that seems unlikely. I'm confused.

