mmlu_pro,"At what wavelength is the spectralemittanceof a blackbody a maximum, if its temperature is a) 500°K, b) 5000°K? c) At what temperature does the maximum spectral emittancelie at a wavelength of 555m\mu, where the eye is most sensitive?","[""a) 500 m\u03bc, b) 5000 m\u03bc, c) 2000 \u00b0K"", ""a) 800 m\u03bc, b) 8000 m\u03bc, c) 4500 \u00b0K"", ""a) 579 m\u03bc, b) 5794 m\u03bc, c) 5000 \u00b0K"", ""a) 600 m\u03bc, b) 6000 m\u03bc, c) 3500 \u00b0K"", ""a) 555 m\u03bc, b) 5550 m\u03bc, c) 5555 \u00b0K"", ""a) 5000 m\u03bc, b) 500 m\u03bc, c) 555 \u00b0K"", ""a) 1000 m\u03bc, b) 10000 m\u03bc, c) 5800 \u00b0K"", ""a) 720 m\u03bc, b) 7200 m\u03bc, c) 3000 \u00b0K"", ""a) 5794m\u03bc, b) 579.4m\u03bc, c) 5220 \u00b0K"", ""a) 2940 m\u03bc, b) 294 m\u03bc, c) 6500 \u00b0K""]",I,"Okay, let's see. The question is about finding the wavelength at which a blackbody's spectral emittance is maximum for different temperatures, and also finding the temperature corresponding to a specific wavelength. I remember there's Wien's Displacement Law that relates the peak wavelength to the temperature.

