Looking at option F: Counterexample when I is true and H and G are false. That would be G=F, H=F, I=T. That's row 7. But in row 7, premise 1 is (G≡H) ∧ ~I. G≡H is T (both false), ~I is ~T=F. So premise 1 is T ∧ F = F. So premises are not both true here. So not a counterexample.

