mmlu_pro,"A 25-year-old gravida 3 para 2 female is admitted to the hospital at 39 weeks' gestation in active labor. She had been having regular contractions every 4 minutes, but is now having only a few intermittent, weak contractions. She has received medication for pain twice in the past 6 hours. Examination shows no reason for obstructed labor. The fetal head is engaged, the membranes are intact, the fetal heart tones are normal, and the cervix is 100% effaced and 7 cm dilated. The most likely diagnosis is","[""hypotonic uterine dysfunction"", ""lower uterine retraction ring"", ""cervical insufficiency"", ""Braxton Hicks contractions"", ""placental abruption"", ""secondary dysfunctional labor"", ""uterine hyperstimulation"", ""premature rupture of membranes"", ""primary dysfunctional labor"", ""cephalopelvic disproportion""]",A,"Okay, let's try to figure this out. The patient is a 25-year-old woman, gravida 3 para 2, so she's had three pregnancies and two deliveries. She's at 39 weeks, which is full term. She was admitted in active labor because she had regular contractions every 4 minutes. But now she's having only a few intermittent, weak contractions. She's had medication for pain twice in the past 6 hours. The exam shows no obstruction, the fetal head is engaged, membranes are intact, fetal heart tones are normal, cervix is 100% effaced and 7 cm dilated.

